    <sectiondef kind="user-defined">
      <header>Energy densities across a Hughes-Winget wrapper</header>
      <description><para>The finite-strain interface reports the elastic energy density and the dissipation per unit <bold>reference</bold> volume, and its hosts store and reload them that way. A wrapped small-strain material knows only its own current volume. A Hughes-Winget wrapper therefore converts both on the way in and multiplies the wrapped material&apos;s updated values by <formula id="1">$ J = \det\boldsymbol{F}^{(n+1)} $</formula> on the way out (toDensityPerReferenceVolume()). The way in differs for the two, because they are different kinds of quantity:</para>
<para><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The <bold>elastic energy</bold> is a function of the state. The wrapped materials update it as <formula id="2">$ \psi_{n+1} = \psi_\mathrm{in} + \psi(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{(n+1)}) - \psi(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{(n)}) $</formula>, so the value handed in must be the density of the state the increment starts from, per unit volume of <formula id="3">$ \boldsymbol{F}^{(n)} $</formula>: the stored value divided by <formula id="4">$ J_n = \det\boldsymbol{F}^{(n)} $</formula> (toDensityPerVolumeAtStartOfIncrement()). The stored result is <formula id="5">$ \Psi_{n+1} = J\,\psi(\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{(n+1)}) $</formula>, the energy of the current state per unit reference volume.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>The <bold>dissipation</bold> is accumulated along the path. Per unit reference volume its rate is <formula id="6">$ J $</formula> times the rate per unit current volume, so <formula id="7">$ D_{n+1} = D_n + J\,\Delta d $</formula>, with <formula id="8">$ \Delta d $</formula> the wrapped material&apos;s increment. The stored value is therefore handed in divided by the same <formula id="6">$ J $</formula> it is multiplied with on the way out (toDensityPerVolumeAtEndOfIncrement()): the round trip leaves the history unchanged and pushes forward only the increment. Dividing by <formula id="9">$ J_n $</formula> instead would rescale the whole history by <formula id="10">$ J/J_n $</formula> in every increment, so that a compaction would decrease it.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>At the first increment <formula id="11">$ \boldsymbol{F}^{(n)} = \boldsymbol{I} $</formula>, and the stored values are zero. An inverted configuration ( <formula id="12">$ J \le 0 $</formula>) makes the energies as meaningless as the stress; all three conversions then pass the value through unchanged, so that a stored value is never divided by zero.</para>
<para>Shared by <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_materials_1_1_hughes_winget_wrapper" kindref="compound">HughesWingetWrapper</ref> and <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_materials_1_1_gradient_enhanced_hughes_winget_wrapper" kindref="compound">GradientEnhancedHughesWingetWrapper</ref>. </para>
</description>
